COVID-19 chest X-rays often show patchy, bilateral, peripheral and lower-lung predominant air-space opacities, from faint hazy reticular increased density to denser focal consolidation; variations include unilateral, multifocal scattered, or diffuse confluent opacities.
COVID-19 chest radiographs frequently show bilateral, patchy air‑space opacities with subpleural peripheral and lower‑zone predominance; variations include unilateral focal consolidation, multifocal confluent consolidation, diffuse reticular change, and occasionally nodular appearances.
COVID-19 chest radiographs often show bilateral peripheral patchy airspace opacities and hazy ground glass attenuation, commonly lower lobe predominant; variations include focal unilateral consolidation, multifocal reticular opacities, and evolving dense lobar consolidation.
COVID-19 chest radiographs often display bilateral, peripheral and basilar‑predominant patchy air‑space opacities, commonly appearing as ground‑glass–like haziness or consolidation; variations include unilateral focal opacities, asymmetric distribution, or extensive confluent consolidation.
In COVID‑19 chest radiographs, images often show bilateral, patchy, peripheral and lower‑zone predominant hazy air‑space opacities, ranging from subtle ill‑defined haziness to confluent consolidation; distribution can be unilateral or multifocal, pleural effusions uncommon.
Chest radiographs commonly show bilateral, peripheral and basilar-predominant patchy air‑space opacities and ground‑glass–like hazy areas, variable in extent, ranging from focal unilateral consolidations to multifocal, confluent peripheral consolidations; pleural effusion is uncommon.
Chest radiographs often show bilateral, patchy, peripheral and lower‑lung predominant hazy opacities ranging from subtle ground‑glass–type reticulonodular haziness to denser focal consolidation; distribution may be multifocal, asymmetric, or occasionally unilateral.
Chest radiographs often show bilateral, patchy air‑space opacities with peripheral or lower‑lung predominance; variations include unilateral focal opacity, diffuse interstitial markings, reticular patterns, multifocal consolidations, and occasional small pleural effusion.
Chest X-ray often shows bilateral, patchy peripheral air‑space opacities with lower‑zone predominance, ranging from faint hazy reticular opacities to confluent consolidation; interstitial markings and asymmetric distribution are common, while pleural effusions are uncommon.
Chest X-ray commonly shows bilateral, peripheral, lower‑lung predominant hazy air‑space opacities and patchy consolidation, ranging from subtle focal opacities to extensive bilateral confluent involvement with occasional reticular change and volume loss.
